## Mechanism

The action engine assumes the old identity's set modules were loaded before
assignment. Ruby's `Card.find` bypasses `Card#initialize`, so it does not load
those modules. `refresh_set_modules` saves `@set_mods_loaded` before assignment
and re-includes only when that flag was already set. The director subsequently
includes modules for the assigned identity. A fetched receiver can retain both
identities; a raw found receiver has only the assigned identity. The
`changing:` event condition examines the receiver's actual singleton ancestry,
not its stored old type. Consequently the Cardtype guard is present for a
fetched Cardtype and absent for the probe's raw found receiver after assignment.

Reference: card 1.110.0 `mod/core/set/all/initialize.rb`,
`mod/core/set/all/assign_attributes.rb`, `lib/card/director/all.rb`, and
`lib/card/set/event/all.rb`. The guard body itself remains unchanged.

Implementation decision: represent direct-found receiver provenance explicitly
on an update. Ordinary updates retain their existing initialized behavior.
The persistence probe must use that provenance for every matching Ruby
`Card.find(...).update!` call, rather than special-casing step 50.

## Changes

- `UpdateCardInput.receiver` distinguishes `find` from `fetch`; omitted means
  the existing initialized/fetched behavior. No HTTP request mapping changed.
- The update validation walk omits the old attachment root only for a raw
  found receiver. The recorded attachment sequence continues into storage
  and integration through the existing mechanism.
- The persistence probe uses `updateFoundCard` for raw found updates. Its
  fetched updates (nested-deck content, account password, and read rule)
  retain initialized behavior. No assertions, oracle rows, skipped-step sets,
  or deliberate-difference declarations changed.
- A Node regression runs the real action engine against an in-memory copy of
  the committed `synthetic-v1` database. It checks saved type, unchanged live
  instances, the subject's action and type-history rows, the default/explicit
  fetched guard, ancestry across the save lifecycle, and the complete serving
  mod runtime. The source database is opened read-only.
- A separate Ruby component control executes the original initialization,
  assignment, pattern-reset, phase-preparation, and event-membership methods.
  Its minimal adapters provide a receiver and two arbitrary pattern modules.
  It does not boot Rails or substitute a production database.

